i'm shelly hall i'm an assistant  professor of paleontology at yale  university and an assistant curator of  invertebrate paleontology at the lp body  museum  66 million years ago an asteroid nearly  as big as Manhattan as long slammed into  the Yucatan Peninsula and it hit so hard  it nearly touched the mantle and threw  dust and ash and aerosols into the sky  and blackened out the Sun the earth got  really cold and really dark and it  rained fire and brimstone from the sky  and in that moment and in the years that  followed most life on Earth went extinct  on land it's really hard to preserve  fossils because everything gets washed  away by the weather but in the ocean on  the other hand everything rains down to  the bottom of the ocean and at the  bottom of the ocean are tons of plankton  and they tell us stories about past mass  extinction events and past abrupt  climate change events and essentially  how the Earth's evolved over a really  long time periods  first way we tried and tackle  question was to figure out the ages of  the rocks but it turns out that what a  volcano does to kill off life is  actually release a lot of nasty gases  and the gases come out at a different  time than the rocks so my group went and  we looked for traces of the gases and  what that allowed us to do was say that  no it really was the impact so 66  million years ago right at the death of  the dinosaurs it was the impact that did  the teeth  by studying past mass extinctions we can  understand what happened and which  species went extinct and where and what  happened afterwards how did things  recover and the hope is that by studying  that we can better inform how we treat  our world today how we protect species  and and make plans for the future that's  bound to be different
 

